 ## Overview
-3D Gaussian Splatting Training is the optimization process that adjusts the parameters of a set of 3D Gaussian primitives to minimize the difference between rendered images and ground truth views [S2][S6, unofficial]. It solves the problem of reconstructing high-fidelity 3D scenes from 2D images by iteratively refining position, rotation, scale, opacity, and appearance (spherical harmonics) attributes [S5, unofficial]. Unlike static initialization methods, this method employs adaptive density control to dynamically manage the topology of the Gaussian set, splitting, cloning, or pruning primitives based on optimization metrics to balance reconstruction quality and computational cost [S2]. It sets itself apart from NeRF-based methods by offering significantly faster training and inference speeds while achieving comparable image quality [S2][S5, unofficial].
+3D Gaussian Splatting Training is the iterative optimization process that adjusts the parameters of a set of 3D Gaussian primitives to minimize the photometric difference between rendered images and ground truth views [S2][S6, unofficial]. It solves the problem of reconstructing high-fidelity 3D scenes from 2D images by converting sparse point clouds into a differentiable radiance field [S5, unofficial]. Unlike NeRF-based methods which rely on implicit neural networks, this method uses explicit Gaussian primitives, offering significantly faster training and real-time rendering speeds while achieving comparable image quality [S2][S5, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from static initialization methods by employing adaptive density control (pruning, splitting, and cloning) to dynamically manage the topology of the Gaussian set during optimization [S2].
 
 ## How it works
